    <string name="primedgun_auto_render_scale_note">The internal EFB follows the game\'s window, 1280x720 on the Quest.</string>
    <string name="primedgun_efb_scale">EFB scale</string>
    <string name="primedgun_efb_scale_note">Scales the internal EFB, which the virtual screen and the visor effects show; higher values use more GPU memory.</string>

    <string name="primedgun_section_advanced">Advanced</string>
    <string name="primedgun_advanced_note">The renderer\'s own switches, which the PC reaches through the F1 overlay. The defaults are the measured best on a Quest 3.</string>
    <string name="primedgun_multiview">Multiview eyes</string>
    <string name="primedgun_multiview_note">Both eyes in one render pass. A Quest 2 or Quest Pro always draws a pass per eye: under multiview its GPU drew some particles as coloured blocks.</string>
    <string name="primedgun_direct_present">Direct to headset</string>
    <string name="primedgun_direct_present_note">The eyes go straight into the headset\'s images; off, through shared buffers on a second device.</string>
    <string name="primedgun_pipelined_rendering">Pipelined rendering</string>
    <string name="primedgun_pipelined_rendering_note">More throughput, one frame of latency. Needs Direct to headset.</string>
    <string name="primedgun_deindex_vertices">Resolve indexed vertices</string>
    <string name="primedgun_deindex_vertices_note">On the CPU, which also keeps the world\'s geometry on the GPU between frames.</string>
    <string name="primedgun_diagnostics_logging">Diagnostics logging</string>
    <string name="primedgun_diagnostics_logging_note">[xr-diag] timings in the log. They cost throughput; leave off to compare performance.</string>

